<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=chemical-revolution">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chemical revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th–18th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as First chemical revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The chemical revolution was the reformulation of chemistry during the 17th and 18th centuries that culminated in the law of conservation of mass and the oxygen theory of combustion, long credited to the French chemist Antoine Lavoisier. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-boyle">Robert Boyle</a> laid its foundations with a corpuscular philosophy drawn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a>, and Joseph Black, Henry Cavendish and Joseph Priestley showed that air is a mixture of gases. Lavoisier, who overthrew the phlogiston theory and stressed precise weighing, published a new nomenclature with colleagues in 1787 and his Traité élémentaire de chimie, its culmination, in 1789.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chemical-revolution.jpg" alt="Chemical revolution" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Louis David · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine-Laurent_Lavoisier_(1743%E2%80%931794)_and_His_Wife_(Marie-Anne-Pierrette_Paulze,_1758%E2%80%931836)_MET_DT1992.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1787</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Antoine Lavoisier · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Recent work sees a gradual change over two centuries, with Robert Boyle laying its foundations.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">Alchemy<span>1st century CE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">Atomic theory<span>1804–1808</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/wohler-synthesis">Organic chemistry<span>1828</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/haber-process">Haber process<span>1909</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=chemical-revolution">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
